{"name":"English Ivy","hex":"#689063","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"english","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Spinning or rotary motion given to a ball around the vertical axis, as in pool, billiards or bowling; spin, sidespin.","An unusual or unexpected interpretation of a text or idea, a spin, a nuance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of English."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/english"},{"term":"ivy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several woody, climbing, or trailing evergreen plants of the genus Hedera.","Any similar plant of any genus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ivy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hedera helix","blocks":[{"text":"Hedera helix, simply ivy, or common ivy, English ivy, European ivy, or King's choice ivy, is a species of flowering plant in the family Araliaceae. It is native to most of Europe and parts of western Asia. Ivy is a clinging evergreen vine that grows on tree trunks, walls, and fences in gardens, waste spaces, and wild habitats. Ivy is common as an ornamental plant, but escaped plants become naturalised outside its native range. Ivy has considerable cultural significance and symbolism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Hedera_helix_Dover.jpg/330px-Hedera_helix_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedera_helix"},{"title":"Hedera","blocks":[{"text":"Hedera, commonly called ivy, is a genus of 12–15 species of evergreen climbing or ground-creeping woody plants in the family Araliaceae, native to Western Europe, Central Europe, Southern Europe, Macaronesia, northwestern Africa and across central-southern Asia east to Japan and Taiwan. Several species are cultivated as climbing ornamentals, and the name ivy especially denotes common ivy, known in North America as \"English ivy\", which is frequently planted to clothe brick walls."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Hedera_algeriensis_kz01.jpg/330px-Hedera_algeriensis_kz01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedera"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Botanical illustration of H. Helix.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Hedera_helix0_correct.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG/500px-Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG/1280px-Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG/500px-Blad_van_klimop_%28Hedera_helix%29_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leaf","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlad_van_klimop_(Hedera_helix)_02.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/%28MHNT%29_Hedera_helix_-_immature_inflorescences.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Hedera_helix_-_immature